<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Captaincy of Pernambuco</h1><p class="st-meta">1534 CE – 1821 CE · capital Olinda</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Brazil's most successful hereditary captaincy, whose sugar plantations made it the world's leading sugar producer in the late 16th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1534 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-of-portugal">King John III</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duarte-coelho">Duarte Coelho</a> the captaincy of Pernambuco, which he called Nova Lusitânia, on the northeastern coast of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a>. Coelho arrived in 1535 and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olinda">Olinda</a>, which became the capital. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caete">Caeté</a> and other indigenous groups and established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> mills with financing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Pernambuco prospered on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> grown on large plantations with mills called engenhos, worked first by enslaved indigenous people and then by enslaved Africans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olinda">Olinda</a> and the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a> were among the richest towns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. From 1630 to 1654 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> occupied Pernambuco as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-brazil">Dutch Brazil</a>. Rivalry between Olinda's planters and Recife's merchants led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-mascates">War of the Mascates</a> in 1710 and 1711.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The crown took direct control in 1654. Pernambuco led the anti-<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/third-portuguese-republic">Portuguese Republic</a> of Pernambuco in 1817, and became a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Empire of Brazil</a> after independence in 1822. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olinda">Olinda's</a> historic centre became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1982.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/captaincy-of-pernambuco.jpg" alt="Captaincy of Pernambuco" width="320" height="308"><figcaption>Image: Floppa Historico · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_do_Brasil_em_1534.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1534 CE – 1821 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Olinda</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Duarte Coelho, Jerónimo de Albuquerque</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Brazil</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nova Lusitânia, Pernambuco</dd></dl></aside></div>

